import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(5,2,"The Technology of Rhythm and Sound","Which do you reach for first when you are wound tight or flat: a push of effort, or a steady rhythm? What would it take to try the other for one week?",[
mc("U","Why, according to Conversation 5.2, does deciding to be calm usually fail while deciding to breathe slowly works?",
 ["Calm is a feeling that cannot be made, while breathing is only a habit that is easy to change.","Slow breathing is a pulse, and every road from scatter to stillness passes through a steady pulse.","Calm needs willpower, and slow breathing needs less of it, so more people can keep it up.","Slow breathing distracts the mind from its worry, and calm then arrives on its own."],1,
 "There is no direct road from scatter to the settled centre. Every path passes through rhythm first, and a breath is a pulse you can set. So you do not aim at calm; you aim at a steady beat, and the beat carries the system the rest of the way.",
 "Scatter cannot jump to stillness; a steady pulse is the only bridge","Part 1"),
mc("A","A man has a speech in an hour and his hands are shaking. Which step fits Conversation 5.2 best?",
 ["Breathe fast and hard for a minute, so that the nervous energy is burned off before he speaks.","Tell himself firmly to calm down, and hold the thought until the shaking stops.","Breathe slowly, about six a minute, with a longer out-breath, for a few minutes.","Sit completely still in silence until his mind has gone quiet, and then begin."],2,
 "Dread is the fast drift, and it needs a rhythm slower than itself. At about six breaths a minute, with the out-breath a little longer, the breath, heart and blood-pressure loop fall into step and the body’s gain settles. Vigorous breathing would add speed to speed.",
 "For the fast drift, slow the breath: about six a minute, longer out","Part 1"),
mc("U","What does the slow breath actually settle, according to Conversation 5.2?",
 ["The body’s gain, the effort and recovery switch, and then attention and mood follow.","The thoughts themselves, taken one at a time, until the mind has nothing left to say.","The filter of habit, which is changed for good by a single long session of breathing.","The sense of self, which dissolves as soon as the breath becomes slow enough."],0,
 "Of all the body’s dials, the gain is the one most directly wired to the breath. Once it settles, attention can hold, the filter of habit loosens and the background worry drops, without extra effort. It is a cascade from one lever.",
 "The slow breath settles the gain, and the rest follows downstream","Part 1"),
judge("“Forceful breathing and breath-holding techniques are as safe to practise alone as the gentle slow breath.”",F,
 "The gentle slow breath is safe for anyone to practise alone. Forceful techniques and retention belong to a wider repertoire that, undertaken without a qualified teacher’s direct guidance, can unsettle the very nervous system the gentle practice steadies. The course teaches only what is safe alone.",
 "Only the gentle slow breath is safe alone; forceful techniques need a teacher","Part 1"),
mc("A","A woman says, “I cannot meditate or repeat a word, because my mind will not stop.” Which reply fits Conversation 5.2?",
 ["Wait until the mind is quiet, since repetition cannot work on a restless mind at all.","Practise anyway: the repetition works below the surface noise and need not wait.","Stop for now, and come back only when she has learned to concentrate well enough.","Repeat the word faster, so that it drowns out the thoughts that are crowding in."],1,
 "The Holy Mother’s advice, as Swamiji gives it, is unconditional: repeat the Name whether the mind is concentrated or not. The practice works at a level below the surface disturbance, and concentration comes later, of itself. A busy mind is no failure.",
 "Practise anyway: the repetition does not wait on a calm mind","Part 2"),
mc("A","A group of young people often feel heavy and flat, and sinking into drowsiness when they sit alone. Which practice fits Conversation 5.2 best?",
 ["Singing together, because the group pulse carries them until their own can catch.","A long silent sitting each morning, in the hope that stillness will lift the heaviness.","A short burst of slow breathing alone, repeated through the day in small sets of ten.","Reading about the mind in a quiet room, in the hope that understanding will wake it."],0,
 "Kīrtana, group singing, brings the singers’ heart rhythms into step. For the heavy drift this matters: a person alone tends only to sink, but the group supplies the rhythmic energy that the lone person cannot generate, and carries them until their own pulse can catch.",
 "For the heavy drift, group singing supplies the energy a lone sitter lacks","Part 2"),
mc("A","A teacher meets a flat, sleepy class after lunch and a wired class before an exam. What does Conversation 5.2 suggest?",
 ["Quiet both of them with the same slow breathing, since calm is what every class needs.","Wake the flat class with movement, and slow the wired class with a rhythm slower than itself.","Wake both with movement, since a class that is awake is easier to teach well.","Leave both alone, since a drift passes of itself and teaching should go on as planned."],1,
 "The heavy drift must be woken first, and the fast drift must be slowed. The wrong rhythm offered to the wrong state is the commonest reason people say a practice does not work for them. Reading the drift comes before choosing the practice.",
 "Wake the heavy, slow the fast: match the rhythm to the drift","Part 3"),
judge("“A mantra is a magic word, and it works because the particular word has a hidden power.”",F,
 "Swamiji presents a mantra as a structured sound, built like a key cut for a lock and like a ladder: a familiar name at the base, a seed-sound above it, the source-sound AUM above that and silence at the top. It is a deliberately built path for attention, not magic, and the Holy Mother’s reminder is that presence matters most.",
 "A mantra is a built ladder of sound, not a magic word","Part 2"),
mc("A","A beginner tries a repeated word silently in the head, and it keeps dissolving into daydream. Which step fits Conversation 5.2?",
 ["Say it aloud or on the lips first, so that the body carries it, and let it go inward later.","Keep it silent, but repeat it more slowly, so that the daydreams have less room to enter.","Give the practice up, since the mind has shown that it is not ready for any repetition.","Skip to the silence at the top of the ladder, where there is no word left to wander from."],0,
 "Vaikharī is the sound spoken aloud, vibrating in the air and heard by the ears. It is the ground floor, to be honoured and not rushed past. The body carries it, and beginners who try to start silently usually find the practice dissolving into daydream.",
 "Begin aloud: the body carries the sound, and silence comes later","Part 2"),
judge("“When you breathe slowly, you are manufacturing a rhythm out of nothing, so the whole effect rests on your effort.”",P,
 "At the level of mechanism your effort is real and necessary: you slow the breath and show up daily. But the deeper view is that the pulse was never absent. You are consenting to a rhythm already running and aligning your small pulse with it. Effort is consent, not force.",
 "The effort is real, but it consents to a rhythm already running","Part 3")])
